<Doc>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>The value comes from</th><th>What reloads it</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>reactive(0)</code></td><td>you</td><td>nothing — you write it yourself</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>reactive(() =&gt; a + b)</code></td><td>other values</td><td>a value its body read, moving</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>{'reactive(() => getThing({ id }))'}</code></td><td>your server</td><td><code>refresh</code>, <code>invalidate</code>, a tag, a <code>ttl</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>{'reactive<Message[]>([], { by: \'id\' })'}</code></td><td>anything that reaches it, live</td><td>nothing — it is appended to</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p><strong>The arrow makes the third row reloadable.</strong>
    <code>{'reactive(getThing({ id }))'}</code> compiles and loads once, and then every trigger on
    it does nothing: a value handed in has no producer to rerun, so <code>refresh</code> and a tag
    alike are inert on it. Where you want one known thing, loaded once and reloaded when you say,
    hand <code>reactive</code> an arrow rather than the call.</p>

    <Code language="ts" caption="shared" code={`const draft = reactive('')
const words = reactive(() => draft().split(' ').length)`} />

